{
  "id": "a0r3qrc",
  "title": "RRB PHYSICS – CHAPTER 6 (DETAILED THEORY)",
  "order": 38,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "3yltkiw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave that requires a material medium to propagate and cannot travel through a vacuum.",
        "The speed of sound is highest in solids, moderate in liquids, and lowest in gases.",
        "Human hearing is sensitive to frequencies between 20 Hz and 20,000 Hz (20 kHz).",
        "An echo requires a minimum distance of 17.2 metres from the reflecting surface at room temperature.",
        "Ultrasonic sound waves (above 20 kHz) are used in SONAR, medical imaging, and industrial cleaning."
      ],
      "id": "eimq1zq"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Audible Range",
          "value": "20 Hz - 20 kHz",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Speed in Air (0°C)",
          "value": "331 m/s",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "Min. Echo Distance",
          "value": "17.2 meters",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Pain Threshold",
          "value": "120 dB",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "njb57jr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "blz9s3r",
      "content": "Sound is a form of mechanical energy that is produced by vibrating bodies and requires a material medium for its propagation."
    },
    {
      "id": "zp6x547",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. NATURE OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "id": "yx80x5d",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "d18bo18",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_d18bo18.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0qjsji4",
      "content": "• Sound is a mechanical wave.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sskkfco",
      "content": "• It is a longitudinal wave."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6vy88bp",
      "content": "• It cannot travel in vacuum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "su0ebty",
      "content": "• It travels in form of compressions and rarefactions."
    },
    {
      "id": "e2t3jb2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. IMPORTANT TERMS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ai1pzbk",
      "content": "• Wavelength (λ): Distance between two consecutive compressions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a7xxb7t",
      "content": "• Time Period (T): Time for one complete vibration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "isbj3ov",
      "content": "• Frequency (f): Number of vibrations per second."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yzivxax",
      "content": "• Amplitude: Maximum displacement of particle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n7xkh3v",
      "content": "• Wave Velocity (v): Distance travelled per second."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wwfy9w7",
      "content": "Relation: v = fλ"
    },
    {
      "id": "12rwxrj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. SPEED OF SOUND IN DIFFERENT MEDIA"
    },
    {
      "id": "32ke0it",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Medium</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Speed (approx.)</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Air (at 20°C)</p>",
            "<p>343 m/s</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Water</p>",
            "<p>1500 m/s</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Steel</p>",
            "<p>5000 m/s</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Vacuum</p>",
            "<p>Cannot travel</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "g2no45d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. CHARACTERISTICS OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "id": "zw1ty2x",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Characteristic</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Depends On</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Meaning</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Loudness</p>",
            "<p>Amplitude</p>",
            "<p>How loud or soft sound is</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pitch</p>",
            "<p>Frequency</p>",
            "<p>High or low sound</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Quality (Timbre)</p>",
            "<p>Waveform</p>",
            "<p>Distinguishes sounds</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s72mm5x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. REFLECTION OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gbxig4q",
      "content": "Sound follows laws of reflection."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uqw4uti",
      "content": "Angle of incidence = Angle of reflection."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2d63ud9",
      "content": "Applications: Echo, SONAR."
    },
    {
      "id": "l8hocn7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. ECHO"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i4wlry6",
      "content": "Echo is repetition of sound due to reflection."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mq72x9a",
      "content": "Minimum distance for echo ≈ 17 m (at 20°C)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "76kwl7k",
      "content": "Time interval for distinct echo ≥ 0.1 second."
    },
    {
      "id": "3zzqe14",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. REVERBERATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kc126ez",
      "content": "Persistence of sound in large halls due to multiple reflections."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ozga03e",
      "content": "Reduced by using curtains, carpets, sound absorbing materials."
    },
    {
      "id": "n9oqdh1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. MULTIPLE REFLECTIONS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lwfqlcm",
      "content": "Examples: Megaphone, Stethoscope, Sound board."
    },
    {
      "id": "97soanf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. SONAR (Sound Navigation and Ranging)"
    },
    {
      "id": "4eso2kh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rvh5xh8",
      "content": "Used to measure depth of sea."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dkrmzo7",
      "content": "Distance = (v × t) / 2"
    },
    {
      "id": "rlouze0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. ULTRASOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ayc470v",
      "content": "Sound frequency &gt; 20,000 Hz."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lixfqy3",
      "content": "Uses: Medical imaging, detecting cracks in metals."
    },
    {
      "id": "azla61h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. SOUND FREQUENCY RANGE"
    },
    {
      "id": "una0wkz",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Frequency Range</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Infrasonic</p>",
            "<p>&lt; 20 Hz</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Audible</p>",
            "<p>20 Hz – 20,000 Hz</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ultrasonic</p>",
            "<p>&gt; 20,000 Hz</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "62nnrjl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. DOPPLER EFFECT"
    },
    {
      "id": "rs58s8r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a15wgfe",
      "content": "Change in frequency due to relative motion between source and observer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rrtbww0",
      "content": "If source approaches → Frequency increases."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n53rswx",
      "content": "If source moves away → Frequency decreases."
    },
    {
      "id": "3wy4itx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. SOUND LEVEL (DECIBEL SCALE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "llaqfg5",
      "content": "Sound intensity level is measured in decibel (dB)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "rualxsf",
      "content": "Normal conversation ≈ 60 dB"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Infrasonic Waves",
        "Audible Waves",
        "Ultrasonic Waves"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Frequency Range",
          "Below 20 Hz",
          "20 Hz to 20,000 Hz",
          "Above 20,000 Hz"
        ],
        [
          "Human Hearing",
          "Inaudible",
          "Audible",
          "Inaudible"
        ],
        [
          "Sources / Applications",
          "Earthquakes, Elephants, Whales",
          "Human speech, Musical instruments",
          "SONAR, Medical Echocardiography, Bats"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "gq8f7st"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f3jn5qc",
      "content": "Above 120 dB → Painful to ear"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "st1yror",
      "content": "0 dB is threshold of hearing."
    },
    {
      "id": "b9the28",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. EFFECT OF TEMPERATURE ON SPEED OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ouwjjh8",
      "content": "Speed of sound in air increases with temperature."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r722qqe",
      "content": "Approximate formula: v = 331 + 0.6T (T in °C)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fpd087n",
      "content": "At 0°C → 331 m/s"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qiamqgd",
      "content": "At 20°C → 343 m/s"
    },
    {
      "id": "q9rlqj3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. RESONANCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zwabyti",
      "content": "Resonance occurs when frequency of external force matches natural frequency of object."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dbdsqz6",
      "content": "It produces large amplitude vibrations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0j2qvv1",
      "content": "Example: Tuning fork, Musical instruments."
    },
    {
      "id": "w3cq86a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. NATURAL FREQUENCY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0q0wthn",
      "content": "Every body has its own natural frequency at which it vibrates freely."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vet7sdb",
      "content": "Depends on length, tension, mass (for strings)."
    },
    {
      "id": "5p8xluh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. BEATS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "py74elj",
      "content": "When two sound waves of slightly different frequencies interfere, beats are produced."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nsv1jlx",
      "content": "Number of beats per second = |f1 - f2|"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "39hs5dz",
      "content": "Common RRB numerical concept."
    },
    {
      "id": "9oza3ya",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. INTERFERENCE OF SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "he1hqfi",
      "content": "When two waves meet, they superpose and form constructive or destructive interference."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "x3zw53l",
      "content": "Constructive → Loud sound"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Speed of sound increases with an increase in temperature and humidity, but remains independent of pressure changes.",
        "Pitch depends on sound frequency, whereas loudness depends on the amplitude of the wave.",
        "The Doppler Effect accounts for the apparent change in pitch when there is relative motion between a sound source and the observer.",
        "Reverberation is reduced in auditoriums by using sound-absorbing materials like coarse plaster or compressed fiberboard."
      ],
      "id": "fra5462"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "pd36wzc",
      "content": "Destructive → Weak sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "y4gcegj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "19. CLASSIFICATION OF MUSICAL INSTRUMENTS"
    },
    {
      "id": "ytsl1m5",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
